/*
Problem: Construct Binary Tree from Inorder and Postorder Traversal (LC 106)
Topic: Binary Tree / Recursion

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Postorder traversal:

Left -> Right -> Root

So the last element of postorder is
always the root of the current subtree.

In inorder traversal:

Left -> Root -> Right

The root divides inorder into:

Left subtree | Root | Right subtree

Since we process postorder from the end,
we must build the RIGHT subtree first,
then the LEFT subtree.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Pick postorder[postIdx] as root.

2. Find its index in inorder.

3. Elements left of that index
   belong to the left subtree.

4. Elements right of that index
   belong to the right subtree.

5. Decrement postIdx.

6. Build right subtree first,
   then left subtree.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Inorder:

[9,3,15,20,7]

Postorder:

[9,15,7,20,3]

Root = 3

Inorder becomes:

[9] | 3 | [15,20,7]

Next root from postorder is 20,
which belongs to the RIGHT subtree.

Hence,
Right subtree is built first.

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Postorder gives the current root.

Inorder tells where to split the tree.

Since we're traversing postorder
backwards (Root → Right → Left),
the right subtree must be constructed
before the left subtree.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n²)

Each search in inorder takes O(n).

(Using a hashmap for inorder indices
reduces it to O(n).)

Space : O(h)

Recursive call stack.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Tree Construction + Divide & Conquer
*/

class Solution {
public:

    // Find root index in inorder
    int search(vector<int>& inorder,
               int left,
               int right,
               int val) {

        for (int i = left; i <= right; i++) {
            if (inorder[i] == val)
                return i;
        }

        return -1;
    }

    TreeNode* helper(vector<int>& postorder,
                     vector<int>& inorder,
                     int& postIdx,
                     int left,
                     int right) {

        if (left > right)
            return NULL;

        // Current root from postorder
        TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(postorder[postIdx]);

        int inIdx = search(inorder,
                           left,
                           right,
                           postorder[postIdx]);

        postIdx--;

        // Build RIGHT subtree first
        root->right = helper(postorder,
                             inorder,
                             postIdx,
                             inIdx + 1,
                             right);

        // Build LEFT subtree
        root->left = helper(postorder,
                            inorder,
                            postIdx,
                            left,
                            inIdx - 1);

        return root;
    }

    TreeNode* buildTree(vector<int>& inorder,
                        vector<int>& postorder) {

        int postIdx = postorder.size() - 1;

        return helper(postorder,
                      inorder,
                      postIdx,
                      0,
                      inorder.size() - 1);
    }
};
